==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=also|:inh|enm:as<id:also>|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂el-|id=all}}{{word|en|ine|swé}}
From {{inh|en|enm|as|as, als(a), alswa}}, from {{inh|en|ang|eallswā||just so; as}}, thus representing a reduced form of {{dbt|en|also|notext=1}}. Compare {{cog|nds-de|a(l)s}}, {{cog|de|als}}, {{cog|nl|als}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{a|en|RP|US|AU|CA}}
** {{IPA|en|/əz/|a=weak form}}
** {{IPA|en|/æz/|[æz]|a=strong form}}
* {{a|en|SSB|Northern England}}
** {{IPA|en|/əz/|a=weak form}}
** {{IPA|en|/æz/|[az]|a=strong form}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Naomi Persephone Amethyst (NaomiAmethyst)-as.wav|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-as.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|æz|s=1}}
<!-- 1 syllable words !-->

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# To such an [[extent]] or [[degree]]; to the same extent or degree.
#: {{ux|en|She regards her kids '''as''' (being) holy as saints.}}
#: {{ux|en|You’re not '''as''' tall as I am, but you are definitely '''as''' happy as can be.}}
#: {{ux|en|It's not '''as''' well made, but it's twice '''as''' expensive.}}
#: {{ux|en|In 1939, '''as''' in 1914, all of Europe seemed to want war.}}
#* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|IV
|passage=“My Continental prominence is improving,” I commented dryly. <br> Von Lindowe cut at a furze bush with his silver-mounted rattan. <br> “Quite so,” he said '''as''' dryly, his hand at his mustache. “I may say if your intentions were known your life would not be worth a curse.”}}
#* {{RQ:Travers Cuckoo in the Nest|chapter=1|passage=She was like a Beardsley ''Salome'', he had said. And indeed she had the narrow eyes and the high cheekbone of that creature, and '''as''' nearly the sinuosity as is compatible with human symmetry. His wooing had been brief but incisive.}}
# [[consider|Considered]] to be, in [[relation]] to something else; in the [[relation]] (specified).
# {{lb|en|formal|somewhat|dated}} [[for example|For example]]; [[for instance]]. {{qualifier|Compare {{m|en|such as}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1820|author=John Strype|title=The Life of the Learned Sir Thomas Smith|page=48
|passage=Likewise many other indulgences were by virtue hereof granted; '''as''', to have a portatile altar, to receive the Sacrament privately; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|chapter=Aboriginal|title=Webster's Unabridged Dictionary
|passage=First; original; indigenous; primitive; native; '''as''', the aboriginal tribes of America.}}
#* {{RQ:Eddison Worm|page=3|passage=The floor of the chamber was tesselated, of marble and green tourmaline, and on every square of tourmaline was carven the image of a fish: '''as''' the dolphin, the conger, the cat-fish, the salmon, the tunny, the squid, and other wonders of the deep.}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|as I live and breathe}}

=====Translations=====
{{see translation subpage|Adverb}}

====Conjunction====
{{en-head|conj}}

# In the (same) way or manner that; to the (same) degree that.
#: {{ux|en|Do '''as''' I say!}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm under a lot of pressure, '''as''' you know.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''As''' you wish, my lord!}}
#: {{ux|en|Mom was slim, '''as''' were most of her relatives.}}
#: {{ux|en|The kidnappers released him '''as''' (was) agreed.}}
#: {{ux|en|A nearby ''to''-infinitive may be a factor for choosing 'in order to', '''as''' will emphasis.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Jason Manning|title=Mountain Honor|publisher=Signet Book|isbn=9780451204806
|passage="But he's good '''as''' dead, and I ain't about to waste a bullet."}}
## {{n-g|Used after {{m|en|so}} or {{m|en||as}} to introduce a [[comparison]].}}
##: {{ux|en|She's twice as strong '''as''' I was two years ago.}}
##: {{ux|en|It's not so complicated '''as''' I expected.}}
##: {{ux|en|It’s as easy to do it right '''as''' it is to do it wrong.}}
## {{n-g|Used to introduce a result:}} with the result that it is; [[as to]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1868|title=Proceedings and Debates of the [New York] Constitutional Convention Held in 1867 and 1868 in the City of Albany|page=2853
|passage=[...] that the Board of Regents had fallen into disrepute; that intelligent men inquired what the board was; he said that it was a quiet body, and kept out of the newspapers — and so quiet '''as''' to lead many to suppose tho board had ceased to exist.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Eric Manasse|title=The Twenty-First Man|publisher=iUniverse|isbn=9780595391288|page=7
|passage=It was a talent he had developed; he could actually be so quiet '''as''' to be practically invisible. In class, he was rarely called upon to answer any questions. In the crowded hallways, he could slip in and out without offending any of the local bullies&nbsp;...}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Herwig C. H. Hofmann; Gerard C. Rowe; Alexander H. Türk|title=Administrative Law and Policy of the European Union|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=9780199286485|page=507
|passage=Under most circumstances, it will be possible to draw a distinction sufficiently clear '''as''' to allow an unambiguous allocation to one or other category.}}
## {{n-g|Expressing concession:}} [[though]].
##: {{ux|en|(As) much '''as''' I respect your viewpoint, I can’t agree.}}
##: {{ux|en|(As) bravely '''as''' they fought, they soon lost the battle.}}
##: {{ux|en|Try '''as''' she might, she could not escape.}}
##* '''1843''' (first published), {{w|Thomas Babington Macaulay}}, ''Essays''
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Matthew Friedman; Laurie B. Slone; J Friedman|title=After the War Zone|isbn=9780786731954
|passage=If this happens, be patient and, difficult '''as''' it may be, try not to take these reactions personally.}}
# At the [[time]] that; during the time when:
## At the same [[instant]] or [[moment]] that: [[when]].
##: {{ux|en|'''As''' I came in, she fled.}}
## At the same [[time]] that, during the same time when: [[while]].
##: {{ux|en|He sleeps '''as''' the rain falls.}}
## Varying through time in the same proportion that.
##: {{ux|en|'''As''' my fear grew, so did my legs become heavy.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''As''' she grew older, she grew wiser.}}
# Being that, considering that, [[because]], [[since]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:because}}
#: {{ux|en|'''As''' it’s too late, I quit.}}
#: {{ux|en|(As) tired '''as''' he was, I decided not to disturb him.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} {{n-g|Introducing a comparison with a hypothetical state (+ subjunctive, or with the verb elided):}} [[as though]], [[as if]]. {{defdate|to 19th century}}
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Acts|2|text=And sodenly there cam a sounde from heven '''as''' it had bene the commynge off a myghty wynde {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|I|1|text=Oft haue I {{long s}}eene the haughty Cardinall, <br> More like a Souldier then a man o' th' Church, <br> As {{long s}}tout and proud '''as''' he were Lord of all {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Spanish Fryar|text=I start '''as''' from some dreadful dream.}}
# {{lb|en|law}} {{n-g|used before a preposition to clarify that the prepositional phrase restricts the meaning of the sentence}}; [[specifically]].
#: {{ux|en|The case is dismissed '''as''' between Jones and Smith.}} (makes explicit that the case is continued between other parties to the litigation)
#: {{ux|en|The case is dismissed '''as''' against Smith.}} (makes explicit that it is continued against some other defendant)
# {{n-g|Functioning as a [[relative]] conjunction, and sometimes like a relative pronoun:}} [[that]], [[which]], [[who]]. {{q|See usage notes.}} {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|You strike me '''as''' being a very fidgety type of person.}}
#: {{ux|en|He had the same problem '''as''' she did getting the lock open.}}
#: {{ux|en|I [[read#Verb|read]] this extract '''as''' being extremely pessimistic.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Romeo and Juliet|text=Now will he sit under a medlar tree,<br>And wish his mistress were that kind of fruit,<br>'''As''' maids call medlars, when they laugh alone.}}
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|part=II|section=5|member=1|subsection=v|passage=the temper is to be altered and amended, with such things '''as''' fortify and strengthen the heart and brain {{...}} }}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Hard Times|[[s:Hard Times/First Book/Chapter II|Book I, Chapter II]]|text=‘Sissy is not a name,’ said Mr. Gradgrind. ‘Don’t call yourself Sissy. Call yourself Cecilia.’<br>‘It’s father '''as''' calls me Sissy, sir,’ returned the young girl in a trembling voice, and with another curtsey.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=w:Alan Moore|title=Jerusalem|publisher=Liveright|year_published=2016|page=99
|passage=“If I had, if I could hold me head up with the better folk, perhaps I'd think again, but I don't reckon '''as''' that's very likely now.”}}
# {{lb|en|rare|now|England|Midland US|and|Southern US|possibly|obsolete}} [[than|Than]].
#* {{RQ:Fuller Church History|text=The king was not more forward to bestow favours on them '''as''' they free to deal affronts to others their superiors.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Use of ''as'' as a relative conjunction meaning "that" dates to late Middle English and was formerly common in standard English<!--see cites-->, but is now only standard in constructions like "the same issue as she had" or "the identical issue as the appellant raised before"; otherwise, it is informal,<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref> found in the dialects of the Midland, Southern, Midwestern and Western US<!--TBD: other regions via DARE-->; and of Lancashire, Cheshire, Shropshire, Staffordshire, Derbyshire, Lincolnshire, Herefordshire, Worcestershire, Warwickshire, Northamptonshire, Huntingdonshire, East Anglia<!--incl. examples from Norfolk-->, Gloucestershire, Oxfordshire, Berkshire, Bedfordshire, Essex, Somerset, Dorset, Wiltshire, Surrey, and Cornwall; sometimes in Durham, Westmorland, Yorkshire and Somerset; only rarely in Northumberland and Scotland; and only in certain set phrases in Nottinghamshire, Leicestershire and Devon.<ref>{{R:Wright}}</ref><!--TBD: Austalian, New Zealand, Canada, India, Ireland.-->

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{l|en|-'s}} {{q|contracted form}}

